    'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Reading someone through a screen is hard. There is no tone of voice, no body language, and one short message can mean many things. Still, there are clear signs someone likes you online, and once you know them you can stop overthinking every reply. This guide explains the signals that show real interest, the mixed signals that deserve a closer look, and the kind way to find out for sure.</p>
<p>One thing to remember from the start: no single sign proves anything. Look for a pattern over several days, not a single message.</p>

<h2>They put in steady effort</h2>
<p>The clearest sign of interest is effort. A person who likes you will make time to talk, even when they are busy. They may not reply instantly, but they do come back. Their messages are more than one word, and they try to keep the chat alive.</p>
<ul>
<li>They reply with real sentences and not just "ok" or "lol".</li>
<li>They start conversations as often as you do.</li>
<li>They say good morning or check in after a long day.</li>
<li>They tell you when they will be busy, so you are not left guessing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Consistency matters more than speed. Someone who replies warmly every day, even if it takes a few hours, is showing more interest than someone who replies in seconds one day and disappears for a week.</p>

<h2>They ask about you and remember the answers</h2>
<p>Curiosity is a big signal. People who like you want to know what your life is like. They ask follow-up questions, and they remember what you told them. If you mentioned an interview on Tuesday and they ask how it went on Wednesday, they were listening.</p>
<p>Look for questions that go beyond small talk, such as "How was your sister's wedding?" or "Did you finish the book you were reading?". Remembering small details is a way of saying "You matter to me" without using those words. If you want to deepen this kind of chat, see our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-start-a-conversation-online">how to start and keep a conversation online</a>.</p>

<h2>They share about themselves</h2>
<p>Interest goes both ways. When someone likes you, they open up gradually. They share stories about their family, their work, their hopes and the funny things that happened to them today. They may send you a photo of their lunch or a meme that made them think of you.</p>
<p>Notice whether they share things that feel personal. Vulnerability, even in small amounts, usually means trust. If a person only answers your questions but never offers anything of their own, they may be shy, or they may simply be less engaged. Time will tell which.</p>

<h2>The signs someone likes you online during busy weeks</h2>
<p>Everyone has hard weeks. The telling thing is how a person behaves when life is busy. Someone who likes you will usually find a small way to stay in touch. They might send a short message like, "Crazy week at work, but I thought of you. Let us catch up on the weekend."</p>
<p>That small gesture tells you a lot. It shows they value the connection enough to protect it. By contrast, someone who vanishes without a word and then returns with no explanation, over and over, is showing you where you sit on their list. Do not make excuses for a pattern that leaves you feeling anxious.</p>

<h2>They move towards meeting you</h2>
<p>Talk is lovely, but action is the strongest sign. A person who likes you will start to suggest more connection. They may say, "I would love to hear your voice," or "We should get coffee when you are free." They might ask about your schedule and suggest a real plan.</p>
<ul>
<li>They suggest a voice or video call.</li>
<li>They propose a specific time and a public place to meet.</li>
<li>They respect your boundaries and do not rush you.</li>
<li>They are happy to meet somewhere busy and in the daytime.</li>
</ul>
<p>Respect for your comfort is just as important as interest itself. Someone who truly likes you will not mind a public place, a friend knowing your plans, or a video call first. If someone pushes for secrecy, private places or quick commitment, be careful. Our <a href="/blog/green-flags-and-red-flags-in-early-dating">green flags and red flags guide</a> explains what to watch for.</p>

<h2>Small signs that add up</h2>
<p>Beyond the big ones, there are quieter signals that tend to show up together when someone is interested. None of them means much alone, but a handful in the same week is encouraging.</p>
<ul>
<li>They react warmly to your photos and updates, and they say something kind and specific.</li>
<li>They use your name, or a friendly nickname you both enjoy.</li>
<li>They tease you gently and laugh at your jokes.</li>
<li>They talk about the future in small ways, such as "we should try that place one day".</li>
