</ul>
<p>Think of these as clues, not proof. People show affection in different ways, and shy people may be warm in tiny details rather than big gestures. Give each person room to be themselves.</p>

<h2>Mixed signals, and what to do about them</h2>
<p>Sometimes the signs point in different directions. They are very warm for two days and then quiet. They flirt but never suggest meeting. They like your photos but give short replies. This can happen for ordinary reasons such as stress, shyness or a busy job. It can also mean someone is keeping their options open.</p>
<p>Here is a calm way to handle it:</p>
<ol>
<li>Watch the pattern for a week or two before deciding.</li>
<li>Match their energy. Do not chase, and do not play games.</li>
<li>Ask a direct, friendly question: "I enjoy chatting with you. Would you like to meet up sometime?"</li>
<li>Pay attention to the answer, and to what they do afterwards.</li>
<li>If the signals stay confusing, move your attention elsewhere.</li>
</ol>
<p>You deserve clarity. Our <a href="/blog/texting-rules-early-dating">texting rules for early dating</a> can help you tell the difference between a busy person and a disinterested one.</p>

<h2>Be careful with signs that are too good</h2>
<p>Real interest grows steadily. Be careful when it arrives suddenly and very strongly. If someone you have never met says they love you within days, calls you pet names immediately, or tells you that you are their destiny, slow down. Add to that any request for money, airtime, gifts or an emergency favour, and it is a serious warning sign.</p>
